akr 0.1
project legeos

record legeos.decision.a-place-is-a-drawn-structure-not-a-richer-rectangle/1 : decision {
    title "A Place's rung is a drawn structure inside its allocation, not a box with more text in it"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/templar-field/**",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/garden.rs",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/surface/design.rs",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs"
    ]
    topic homegarden-form
    decision """
        The layout engine keeps allocating rectangles; what is drawn inside one is not a card. A Place renders the structure its own use has earned, and the whitespace inside its allocation is part of that form:

        - development/research → a branch structure: the root, the descendants that actually mattered, drawn as a tree with the heavier branches weightier;
        - media → a horizontal shelf;
        - document-heavy work → a loose folio/stack;
        - a plain folder → stays a plain simple form.

        The visual grammar is five dimensions and no more: **position = memory, size = relevance, shape = mode of use, material = identity, colour = state**. Enough to be rich; few enough not to become decorative noise.

        Scale differences are aggressive once blooms exist. A lightly relevant place is a line — `[ Downloads · 2 new ]` — while a context that has dominated several sessions may take about a quarter of the usable field and expose its actual branch or shelf structure. "Same rectangle with more text" is not a representation stage. The test is whether a person can glance at Home from two metres and see which places currently matter.

        The field has garden zones without hard sections: no `Projects`/`Recent`/`System` headings, only weak spatial tendencies. New grown objects land in one broad open landing region so first growth is not scattered across the canvas; manually placed objects keep their coordinates forever; a grown object regrows near where it last landed.

        The substrate stays very quiet: a slightly warmer, mineral off-white, no aggressive darkening or decoration. Material texture appears only on established Places, never on the field. State colour stays scarce — neutral almost everywhere, with amber/red/grey carrying genuinely exceptional meaning.
        """
    context """
        Outside review of the Homegarden plan, 2026-09-08, brought by the user. The plan described Small/Wide/Large as progressively richer rectangles, which is the same failure the bottom bar had: more text in a bigger box is not a different representation. The reviewer's alternative keeps rectangular allocation for the layout engine — so `lege_field`'s ops, inverses and reflow are untouched — and changes only what is painted inside one. This also settles the geometry axis the earlier plan deferred: shape carries mode of use, so Wide-vs-Tall stops being a separate problem.

        The branch body already exists in a first form (`shell/lege-shell/src/garden.rs::branch_rows`, drawn by `paint_branches` at the Large rung), so this is the direction that work was already pointing; what is new is the shelf and folio forms, the landing region, the aggressive scale range and the quiet substrate.
        """
    consequences """
        Not yet built. It supersedes nothing: `@legeos.decision.homegarden-activity-grown-places` keeps every rule about how a Place is earned, aged and positioned, and this record only governs what one looks like. The Wide-vs-Tall geometry axis that homegarden.md lists as deliberately absent is now answered by `shape = mode of use` rather than by a `Representation::for_span` rewrite. Needs: a mode classifier per context (development / media / documents / plain), a shelf and a folio renderer beside the existing branch renderer, a landing region in the tender, a wider stage range, and a substrate colour change in `design`. The blooms this depends on for the small end (`2 new`) are still unbuilt.
        """
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.homegarden-activity-grown-places ]
}

record legeos.decision.agplv3-own-code/1 : decision {
    title "D13/ADR-0013 — own code is AGPL-3.0-only; lege-comp stays GPL-3.0-only (§13 combination); Silvaview-rs relicensed identically"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "Cargo.toml",
        path "LICENSE",
        path "debian/copyright",
        path "deny.toml",
        path "docs/adr/0013-agplv3.md",
        path "interfaces/wayland/**"
    ]
    decision """
        Every first-party crate, interface, document and asset is AGPL-3.0-only (repository-wide LICENSE, workspace license field, deny.toml allow-list, Wayland <copyright> lines, cargo xtask copyright default, full text in DEP-5 debian/copyright because Ubuntu's common-licenses has no AGPL-3). compositor/lege-comp stays GPL-3.0-only for ADR-0012's reason; GPLv3 §13 and AGPLv3 §13 permit the combination with each part keeping its licence. Forks keep upstream licences. Silvaview-rs (the author's SequoiaView port, previously unlicensed) is relicensed AGPL-3.0-only so its pure squarify/colour/cushion modules can be lifted into crates/lege-treemap.
        """
    context """
        The Home Field work lifts code from Silvaview-rs; the author chose to put both projects under AGPL-3.0 rather than keep LegeOS on MPL-2.0 (ADR-0008) and relicense only the lifted modules. One strong copyleft also removes ADR-0012's asymmetry of one crate under a stronger licence than the crates it links.
        """
    consequences """
        cargo deny allows AGPL-3.0 for own code and MPL-2.0 for dependencies only; contributions accepted under AGPL-3.0-only, no CLA; network use must offer source (§13).
        """
    depends_on [ @legeos.decision.compositor-on-smithay ]
    implements [ @legeos.constraint.packaging-and-render-thread ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.decision.toolchain-licence-namespace ]
}

