record legeos.decision.window-groups-are-explicit-and-compositor-owned/1 : decision {
    title "Window groups are explicit, owned by templar-window, stacked as one, placed beside on grouping, outlined as one, and one chip on the island; Alt+Tab across every window, Alt+` within the unit, Super+Tab opens Taskfield"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "compositor/templar-comp/**",
        path "crates/templar-field/**",
        path "crates/templar-window/**",
        path "interfaces/wayland/**",
        path "shell/templar-shell/**"
    ]
    topic window-groups
    decision """
        A window group is an explicit, user-made set of two or more application windows that behave as one unit of work. Seven rules: (1) groups are explicit — made by dropping one window's title band on another's band, or by the Super+G chord; never inferred from proximity, application or time. (2) The compositor owns groups through WindowService: membership lives in templar-window, the shell learns it over templar_shell_v1 (toplevel v3 `group` event), so every switcher works with the shell dead. (3) A group is one thing to stack: activating any member raises every member contiguously, relative order kept, the activated one on top; geometry never changes. (4) Chords, revised 2026-09-09 by @legeos.decision.task-system-four-scales-one-model: Alt+Tab / Alt+Shift+Tab walk the seven most recently activated windows across every unit and output, never scoped to a group (the original scoping, withdrawn 2026-09-08, made a group a place Alt+Tab could not leave). Alt+` / Alt+Shift+` walk the current task unit — the group's members when the focused window is grouped, the application's windows otherwise — so the group feature still costs nothing until a group exists. Super+Tab opens Taskfield, whose sections are task units with explicit groups first, so 'across groups' is subsumed rather than lost; there is no separate group-cycle chord any more. Both switchers walk a list frozen at the first press and released with the modifier (templar_window::SwitchRing), because every step activates and activation raises. (5) A group has at least two members; it dissolves when the second-to-last leaves or closes, and its chip goes with it. (6) Grouping places, it never merges: the dropped or chorded window is set down beside the target on the nearest free side, edge to edge; nothing is stacked, tabbed or folded into one frame; only the band is a drop target; a band drag moves the whole group and Shift+drag moves one member; in Taskfield, dropping a card on another section groups the same way. (7) A group is visibly one thing: a two-pixel outline in the group's accent around the union of the members' rectangles, the same colour as a stripe in each member's band, its island chip and its Taskfield section; the accent is assigned from a short palette by group id in templar-window. Super+G groups with the previously focused window; Super+Shift+G ungroups. There is no Ctrl+Tab binding and no setting for any of this. A group appears once on the island, keyed `group:<id>` — never on Home.
        """
    context """
        The user asked on 2026-09-03 for grouped windows that can be dragged together as one window group, with Alt+Tab switching inside a group and Ctrl+Tab between groups. On reading the plan the user chose Super+Tab outright (Ctrl+Tab belongs to tabbed applications), and specified that dropping one window on another must place it beside the other rather than combine them, with a coloured outline around the whole group as the indicator.

        Revised 2026-09-08: the Home half was withdrawn — a running window, and so a group, is work in progress and lives on the bar and nowhere else.

        Revised 2026-09-09: the Task System decision gives Super+Tab to Taskfield. Cycling groups by their last-active member was one linear list too many; Taskfield's top level already shows every unit with groups first, and the within-unit walk that a group needs is Alt+`. WindowService::group_cycle_targets and the window.group-cycle-* actions are deleted; window.within-unit-forward/backward take their place with the fan as the pointer route.
        """
    consequences """
        Plan in docs/planning/window-groups-and-application-integration.md §2–3 and @legeos.work.window-groups (WG1 model, WG2 compositor, WG3 protocol, WG5 hands-on); the chord swap and the Taskfield drop-to-group are in @legeos.work.task-system. The existing Super+Left/Right spatial step keeps its stored-nothing semantics as window.step-right/left. Applications keep Ctrl+Tab. The group accent palette becomes per-theme when the two themes land (@legeos.decision.appearance-is-a-working-field-with-two-themes). From the 2026-09-08 revision: WG4's Home tile is superseded by the island chip (`taskbar::Entry::key` is `group:<id>`); the fan is the successor consumer of the parked and group tiles' drawing.
        """
    implements [ @legeos.decision.home-is-momentary-and-placeable-objects-are-containers ]
}

record legeos.decision.window-tabs-are-external-revealed-and-stationary/1 : decision {
    title "A window's tabs live outside it on one chosen side, appear when the pointer asks, never resize the client and never move during an interaction"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/chrome/**",
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/input.rs",
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/space_sync.rs",
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/xdg_shell.rs",
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/xwayland.rs",
        path "crates/templar-session-core/src/settings.rs",
        path "crates/templar-window/**",
        path "interfaces/wayland/templar-shell-v1.xml"
    ]
    topic window-tabs
    decision """
        Application pixels belong to the application; a window's name tag and its controls (Park, Maximize, Close) belong to the compositor and live outside the client, on one side of it. Five rules.
        (1) One side per window, from TabSide {Top, Bottom, Left, Right}. The default comes from a setting (windows.tabs.side, default top: the owner's hand reaches for the top). A window's side changes only by an explicit act (a per-window choice from the name tag) or, when windows.tabs.follow-resize is on, from the latched edge of a resize grab, applied after the grab ends. Never from instantaneous pointer direction.
        (2) Stationary: the tabs have a fixed anchor on their side. They never follow the pointer, and while one is pressed or a drag/resize grab runs, side and visibility are latched until the grab ends.
        (3) Revealed, not permanent (windows.tabs.reveal = hover by default, always as the discoverable fallback). Hidden tabs take no input: the exterior band where they would appear belongs to whatever is under it until the pointer rests there and the tabs are drawn. A reveal never activates anything; Close needs its own click on a visible Close. Leaving has a short grace. A window's hidden tabs never take input from a window stacked above it at that point.
        (4) Tabs never reconfigure the client. The client rectangle is the window's geometry; showing, hiding or changing side of the tabs sends no configure and changes no client size. One authoritative layout from WindowService (client, side, name and control rectangles, reveal band, resize zones, recovery target) is what chrome drawing, press routing, dwell, recovery and damage all read.
        (5) Resize owns the corners. The exterior corner squares of every window are resize zones whatever side its tabs are on, and the tabs are inset from them, so Close and resize never compete for one press location.
        Where there is no exterior room on the chosen side (a window flush with the output edge, maximized, fullscreen) the tabs are drawn over the client's own edge on a dwell, as covering_chrome does for a screen-filling window today; that is an overlay and is named as one, not external.
        """
    context """
        Today the tags are a permanent 32 px strip fixed to the bottom of the frame and subtracted from the client (geometry::client_area, tag_strip), Close sits under the client's visible bottom-right corner (@legeos.observation.close-tag-sits-under-the-client-bottom-right-corner), and the owner wants tabs that can appear on any side, possibly chosen by resizing, possibly revealed on mouseover. The 2026-10-01 advisor recommended stationary anchors with exterior hover reveal as the default, resize-chosen placement only as a latched, post-grab hint, and exhausting top/bottom before vertical sides. Open for the owner before this leaves proposed: (a) default side top versus bottom; (b) reveal trigger, a dwell in the exterior band versus immediate entry; (c) a discoverability gesture that shows every tab and resize zone at once (bare Super is taken by Home reveal); (d) how Left/Right sides arrange a horizontal name tag (rotated text is ruled out).
        """
    consequences """
        geometry::client_area, frame_around and tag_strip as the frame/strip relationship are removed in a clean cutover; MIN_WINDOW_HEIGHT and the recovery rules are restated against the tab side. A window gains 32 px of client height at the same geometry. is_recoverable/repair_window guarantee a reachable tab anchor on the chosen side or the overlay fallback. The bottom-centre gesture gate and the bottom-edge peek keep priority over a bottom-side reveal band at the output's bottom edge. Amends the tags half of @legeos.decision.one-persistent-bottom-bar-tags-not-bands (tags stay, the bottom strip goes).
        """
    supported_by [ @legeos.observation.close-tag-sits-under-the-client-bottom-right-corner ]
    author "dk"
    source {
        kind external
        role rationale
        document "external-window-tabs-advice-20261001"
        use """
            Stationary anchors, exterior hover reveal, latched resize hint, separate resize and control zones, one authoritative layout, no client reconfigure, the fullscreen overlay constraint.
            """
    }
}
